when we loanch the app (in page1), the sidebar is closed, when we navigate to page2, sidebar opens, but when we go back in page1, even if the "closed" part of the code runs, the sidebar stays open.

Two adjustments I've recently used in an app are:

1. Have the `value` of a `showcase_layout = "top right"` value box take up the entire value box width with

   ```css
   .bslib-value-box.showcase-top-right .value-box-grid .value-box-area {
     grid-column: 1 / 3;
   }
   ```

2. Use the container query width to drive the `value` font-size, rather than the viewport width (it currently uses something like `font-size: 1.325rem + .9vw`).

   ```css
   .bslib-value-box .value-box-value {
     font-size: calc(1rem + 3cqi);
   }
   ```

   With this rule, the width of the value box itself drives the font size of the value, so the value shrinks and grows proportional to the value box width.

A rule like this is helpful to ensure that opening the sidebar doesn't overly squish the main content

```css
@media (min-width: 576px) {
  .bslib-page-sidebar > .bslib-sidebar-layout.html-fill-item > .main {
    min-width: max(576px, 100%);
  }
}
```

This rule isn't quite perfect because the scrolling happens across the entire content area and not the main sidebar area, but it's a reasonable starting place.

