What we currently have for multi-column layouts (i.e., `layout_column_wrap()`) is great when each column is of the same width. And, although [varying column widths is possible](https://rstudio.github.io/bslib/articles/layouts.html#varying-widths), it's not easy. We _could_ make it easier, but even if we do, it's still not clear how we'd enable _responsive_ varying widths (i.e., changing widths at different breakpoints), and it wouldn't be as simple as the 12-column system.

Responsive varying widths has always been a selling point of Bootstrap grid, and folks are bound to reach for `fluidRow()`/`column()` to get multiple columns in a fill/fillable setting. Unfortunately, since modern Bootstrap grid (the one based on flexbox essentially assumes `flex-direction: row`, which makes it incompatible with the fill/fillable framework).

Probably the best way around this is to introduce a new version of `fluidRow`/`column()` that's based on the new CSS Grid powered grid layout system https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/layout/css-grid/. 

NOTE: the CSS grid powered grid is currently flagged as experimental, so we should probably wait on wrapping it, but it could look something like this:

```r
library(bslib)

layout_column_grid <- function(widths, ..., fill = TRUE, fillable = TRUE) {
  div(
    class = "grid",
    !!!Map(
      f = function(w, x) {
        div(
          class = paste0("g-col-md-", w), 
          class = "g-col-12", 
          x
        ) |>
          bindFillRole(container = fillable)
      },
      widths, list2(...)
    )
  ) |>
    bindFillRole(item = fill)
}

page_fluid(
  theme = bs_theme(
    "enable-grid-classes" = "true",
    "enable-cssgrid" = "true"
  ),
  card(
    full_screen = TRUE,
    layout_column_grid(
      c(4, 8),
      plotly::plot_ly(),
      plotly::plot_ly()
    )
  )
)
```



(Also note that `widths` could take a value like `list(md = c(4, 8), sm = c(2, 10))`)

